  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The offense never showed up. Zero runs on one hit, and the defense that usually holds tight got overwhelmed early when Max Fried lasted just three innings. Baltimore scored seven before the Yankees found their way into the box score, let alone the scoreboard. By the time anyone was swinging seriously, the game was already decided.",
    "what_this_means": "This is what happens when pitching gives you a chance and the lineup refuses to take it. Fried's early exit meant the bullpen had to bridge five innings, and while Yarbrough kept the bleeding light in the backend, the damage was done by then. You don't win games on one hit and seven strikeouts. The problem isn't that they were unlucky. It's that they didn't execute when they had to, left five runners on base across two critical innings, and handed the game away before anyone had to come back from anything. This team is 27\u201317 because they usually finish. Today they didn't.",
    "what_to_watch": "Clay Holmes takes the mound for the Mets tonight with a 1.86 ERA, and Cam Schlittler counters with a 0.71 mark over his last four starts. The matchup is clean and tight, and a shutdown starter on either side means runs will matter more than volume.",
